

LOGIN(1)            UNIX Programmer's Manual            LOGIN(1)



NAME
     login - authenticate and start a session

SYNOPSIS
     login [-T type] [ name ]

DESCRIPTION
     Login authenticates a user against /etc/passwd and, on
     success, replaces itself with that user's shell.  It expects
     the terminal line on file descriptors 0, 1 and 2, which is
     how getty(8) invokes it; the optional name argument is the
     login name getty has already read and echoed.

     Without an argument (or on any retry) login prompts:

          login:

     and then, always:

          Password:

     The password is read with echo off.  A password is asked for
     even when the account does not exist -- answering "no such
     user" immediately would tell an attacker which names are
     real.  Three attempts are allowed; each failure prints

          Login incorrect

     and re-prompts.  After the third, login exits 1 and init(8)
     starts a fresh getty on the line.

     An empty line, or a login name longer than 63 characters, is
     discarded and the prompt repeated.  This costs no attempt: it
     is a malformed line, not a failed authentication.  Ten such
     lines in one session do end it, so holding Return down cannot
     hold the terminal open forever -- upstream relies on its
     alarm(3) login timeout for that, which this port does not
     have.

     On success login sets the group and user id from the passwd
     entry, chdir()s to the home directory (failure is not fatal),
     and execs the shell named there -- /bin/sh if the field is
     empty -- with argv[0] set to a hyphen followed by the last
     component of the shell's path, e.g. "-sh".  The leading
     hyphen is what makes it a login shell.

ENVIRONMENT
     The exec'd shell receives exactly five variables; nothing is
     inherited from getty:

          PATH=:/bin:/usr/bin:/sbin
          HOME       the passwd entry's directory field
          SHELL      the shell being exec'd
          USER       the login name
          TERM       the type given by -T if there is one;
                     otherwise the ty_type field for this line
                     from /etc/ttys, or vt100 if the line is not
                     listed there.  The pty slaves are listed as
                     fgxpty, which is right for a session from the
                     FGX console and wrong for anything else --
                     hence -T, which telnetd(8) fills in from the
                     client's own TERMINAL-TYPE negotiation and
                     passes down through getty(8)

SECURITY
     Root may log in only on a line marked secure in /etc/ttys
     (see ttys(5)).  A line that is not listed in /etc/ttys at all
     is not treated as insecure -- refusing root on every line the
     file does not mention would lock the only account out of
     anything newly added.  The pty slaves are therefore listed
     explicitly: ttyp0 is secure and ttyp1 is not, which is what
     makes this gate a path that can be tested rather than code
     that has never run.

     Passwords are stored hashed, in the tagged form

          $f$xy$hhhhhhhh

     where xy is a two-character salt and hhhhhhhh is eight hex
     digits.  Login dispatches on the $ tag to recover the salt,
     never on the string's length, so a second hash format can be
     added later without changing stored entries.

     THE THREAT MODEL IS DELIBERATELY LIMITED.  This hash is not
     DES; see crypt(3).  It stops a shoulder-surfer and a casual
     reader of /etc/passwd.  It is not a defence against an
     attacker who has the file and wants the password.

     An account whose password field is empty cannot be logged
     into at all.  Traditional BSD treats an empty field as "no
     password required"; FGX fails closed instead, so a truncated
     or half-written /etc/passwd cannot open an account.

DIAGNOSTICS
     login: /bin/tcsh: out of memory, falling back to /bin/sh
          The shell named in the passwd entry could not be
          exec'd for want of bank-0 stack arena, so login retried
          with /bin/sh, which costs 12 pages against tcsh's 55.
          The session starts; it is simply not the requested
          shell.  Losing the shell would otherwise drop the user
          back to a login prompt they cannot get past.  The
          fallback is skipped when /bin/sh is already the shell
          that failed, so this message never names /bin/sh.

FILES
     /etc/passwd           user database
     /etc/ttys             line database (TERM, secure flag)

SEE ALSO
     getty(8), telnetd(8), ttys(5), passwd(5), crypt(3)

BUGS
     There is no lastlog, wtmp or utmp, so nothing records who
     logged in or when, and who(1) has nothing to read.  There is
     no login timeout: a prompt left unanswered waits forever.
     Quotas, login classes and supplementary groups do not exist
     on FGX and the corresponding upstream code is deleted rather
     than stubbed.



